Sval: count a window's visible tabs only where the tab bar update uses it

TabBarVisibility.update runs on every tab close and counted the
window's visible tabs each time, a filter over every tab once the close
had dropped tabbrowser's cache of them. Only a popup or taskbar-tab
window reads the count, so closing a thousand tabs in an ordinary
window walked the strip a thousand times for nothing. Sval releases a
Workspace's tabs this way on every switch away from it.

diff --git a/browser/components/tabbrowser/content/tabbrowser.js b/browser/components/tabbrowser/content/tabbrowser.js
index db6d409597..68c2823558 100644
--- a/browser/components/tabbrowser/content/tabbrowser.js
+++ b/browser/components/tabbrowser/content/tabbrowser.js
@@ -10150,8 +10150,10 @@ var TabBarVisibility = {
       Services.prefs.getBoolPref("sidebar.verticalTabs", false);
 
     // When `gBrowser` has not been initialized, we're opening a new window and
-    // assume only a single tab is loading.
-    let hasSingleTab = !gBrowser || gBrowser.visibleTabs.length == 1;
+    // assume only a single tab is loading. Sval: only a single tab window
+    // asks, and counting the visible tabs walks every tab on each close.
+    let hasSingleTab =
+      isSingleTabWindow && (!gBrowser || gBrowser.visibleTabs.length == 1);
 
     // To prevent tabs being lost, hiding the tabs toolbar should only work
     // when only a single tab is visible or tabs are displayed elsewhere.
